<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The Tour de France began with night riding, hidden assistance, fanatical spectators and a 1904 scandal that nearly ended the race."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1900-1920/rytter-ved-maal.webp"/><title>Cheating, nails and chaos at the Tour de France · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/snyd-soem-tour-de-france-1904/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero"><div><p class="section-label">Story · Tour de France 1904</p><h1>Cheating, nails and a Tour de France in <em>chaos</em></h1></div><p>When road racing acquired its greatest race, rules already existed. The problem was enforcing them across hundreds of kilometres of dark road.</p></header><figure class="wildwest-hero-image second"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1900-1920/rytter-ved-maal.webp`} alt="An exhausted cyclist being helped at the finish"/><figcaption>The finish could resemble a rescue operation. In the pioneering years, exhaustion was part of the race’s story.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead"><p class="section-label">The Tour begins</p><p>The first Tour de France in 1903 consisted of six enormous stages and more than 2,400 kilometres. Several stages began or ended at night. Officials waited at selected checkpoints, but much could happen between them.</p></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Between the checkpoints</p><h2>Rules on an empty road</h2></div><div><p>Riders were accused of holding on to cars, taking trains, receiving spare parts or having friends send rivals in the wrong direction. Spectators were not neutral either. Local heroes had fanatical supporters who might block the road or threaten rivals.</p><p>This does not mean everyone cheated freely. A race official simply could not watch the whole route. Many decisions relied on witnesses, rumours and conflicting accounts. A rider could be pushed uphill by the crowd and later claim that he had tried to break free.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">Tour de France 1904</p><h2>The scandal that nearly killed the race</h2></div><div><p>The second Tour descended into chaos. On the Col de la République, spectators tried to help local rider Antoine Fauré by blocking and attacking his rivals. Nails were scattered elsewhere, and several riders were suspected of taking trains or receiving extensive hidden assistance.</p><p>Maurice Garin reached Paris first, but after months of investigation the French cycling federation disqualified the first four: Maurice Garin, Lucien Pothier, César Garin and Hippolyte Aucouturier. Stage winners also lost their results.</p><p>Nineteen-year-old Henri Cornet was declared the winner and remains the Tour’s youngest overall winner. The exact offences behind every disqualification were never fully published. Organiser Henri Desgrange angrily declared that the Tour was finished — but it returned the following year with revised rules and tighter control.</p></div></section>
<figure class="wildwest-wide poster"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1900-1920/wild-west/street-race-poster.webp`} alt="Danish newspaper advertisement for a street race at a sports festival" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Cycle racing soon became part of popular festival culture. This later Danish advertisement shows a street race promoted alongside music, football and other displays.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Rider and mechanic</p><h2>Alone with a broken bicycle</h2></div><div><p>One of the harshest ideals of the pioneering period was that riders repaired their own bicycles. The best-known example came in the 1913 Tour when Eugène Christophe broke his fork on the descent from the Col du Tourmalet.</p><p>He walked several kilometres with the bicycle on his shoulder to a forge in Sainte-Marie-de-Campan and repaired the fork himself under supervision. When a boy helped with the bellows, Christophe was even penalised for accepting assistance. A champion had to be cyclist, mechanic, blacksmith and rescuer.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">From circus to sport</p><h2>Suffering became part of the product</h2></div><div><p>The major races were created by newspapers, bicycle manufacturers, velodrome owners and promoters seeking to sell newspapers, tickets and bicycles. Five hundred kilometres, a night on dreadful roads or six days on a wooden track showed that both human and machine could pass familiar limits.</p><p>Rules became clearer, control improved and assistance grew more organised. Yet the image of the solitary, suffering cyclist survived. In 1924, journalist Albert Londres called Tour riders <em>les forçats de la route</em> — the convicts of the road. The phrase came later, but captured the culture created by the pioneering races.</p><p>The sport has become more civilised. Even so, the Wild West can still be glimpsed when riders hit the cobbles of Paris–Roubaix or continue after a crash. Somewhere beneath carbon fibre, team radios and electronic gears, the pioneering years remain.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Editorial note</p><div><p>This story brings together the period’s technical and human development. Some famous anecdotes survive in several versions; uncertainty is stated where the documentation is incomplete.</p></div></section><nav class="wildwest-next" aria-label="Article navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/cykelsportens-vilde-vesten/`}>← Previous story</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1900-1920/`}>Period page →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
